00:00:30.440 --> 00:00:32.420
SAPTARSHI MUKHERJEE: Yeah.

00:00:32.420 --> 00:00:35.100
And this topic is
about human emotions,

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:37.950
and how do you understand
human emotions,

00:00:37.950 --> 00:00:40.889
apply algebra to
determine human emotions.

00:00:40.889 --> 00:00:42.680
And we're going to talk
about research work

00:00:42.680 --> 00:00:45.826
by Dr. Robert Plutchik
in this field.

00:00:45.826 --> 00:00:47.450
But we're going to
talk about how do we

00:00:47.450 --> 00:00:49.940
apply emotion
arithmetic in business

00:00:49.940 --> 00:00:52.941
and make it real to
delight our customers?

00:00:52.941 --> 00:00:54.440
In that context,
we're going to talk

00:00:54.440 --> 00:00:56.930
about Google Cloud's Data
Analytics and Data Science

00:00:56.930 --> 00:00:57.836
Platform.

00:00:57.836 --> 00:00:59.460
And then we're going
to do a real demo.

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:00.730
We're going to do
real coding, Eric?

00:01:00.730 --> 00:01:01.430
ERIC SCHMIDT: We are, yes.

00:01:01.430 --> 00:01:03.013
SAPTARSHI MUKHERJEE:
OK, that's great.

00:01:03.013 --> 00:01:05.450
Let's hope it all goes well.

00:01:05.450 --> 00:01:08.060
With that, let's
talk, about love.

00:01:08.060 --> 00:01:11.240
Love has been one of
the most argued topics

00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:12.500
that I can ever remember.

00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:15.890
And I think that's true
for many, many decades

00:01:15.890 --> 00:01:16.850
and centuries.

00:01:16.850 --> 00:01:19.610
We have great poets
who have defined loves.

00:01:19.610 --> 00:01:22.700
We have great philosophers,
and most recently

00:01:22.700 --> 00:01:25.270
evolutionary biologists,
talking about love

00:01:25.270 --> 00:01:27.270
as the evolutionary necessity.

00:01:27.270 --> 00:01:30.590
So it's an interesting topic,
top of mind for many people.

00:01:30.590 --> 00:01:33.560
And we have been discussing this
in the context of philosophy,

00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:35.630
poet, psychology.

00:01:35.630 --> 00:01:40.370
And we're going to define "love"
is equal to trust plus joy.

00:01:40.370 --> 00:01:42.300
And how do we come
to this equation?

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:43.580
So that's the fun.

00:01:43.580 --> 00:01:46.550
Let's get started.

00:01:46.550 --> 00:01:48.470
The psychologists
have been trying

00:01:48.470 --> 00:01:51.380
to understand love and
human emotions for decades.

00:01:51.380 --> 00:01:53.840
And one of the most
cited work in this space

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:56.090
is from Dr. Robert
Plutchik, who came up

00:01:56.090 --> 00:01:58.970
with this very interesting
model of human psychology called

00:01:58.970 --> 00:02:00.770
the "Wheel of Emotion."

00:02:00.770 --> 00:02:04.880
And what it does, it allows us
to come with resultant emotion

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:07.030
based on underneath
nuanced emotions.

00:02:07.030 --> 00:02:09.139
So for example,
as for his model,

00:02:09.139 --> 00:02:12.050
love equals to
trust plus joy, awe

00:02:12.050 --> 00:02:15.530
equals to fear plus
amazement, and then we

00:02:15.530 --> 00:02:18.210
have contempt, which
is anger plus disgust.

00:02:18.210 --> 00:02:20.070
But let's look at the model.

00:02:20.070 --> 00:02:22.250
The way to interpret
this model is

00:02:22.250 --> 00:02:25.100
you have an ordinal
listing of human emotions

00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:26.330
throughout this model.

00:02:26.330 --> 00:02:30.110
And the emotions which are
at the very central piece

00:02:30.110 --> 00:02:32.340
of this model are the
most intense ones.

00:02:32.340 --> 00:02:35.720
If you see admiration,
terror, amazement, grief,

00:02:35.720 --> 00:02:38.000
these are very intense
human emotions.

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:42.410
And as you try to move outside
of the wheel across the petals,

00:02:42.410 --> 00:02:44.960
the intensity of those
emotions come down.

00:02:44.960 --> 00:02:49.130
And if you add the two emotions
in the adjacent petals,

00:02:49.130 --> 00:02:51.410
you get the resultant emotion
in between the petals.

00:02:51.410 --> 00:02:56.180
So for example, if we zoom
into this particular aspect

00:02:56.180 --> 00:03:01.040
of the wheel, joy plus
trust is equal to love.

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:04.410
And similarly, if
we see awe, it's

00:03:04.410 --> 00:03:06.440
a state of fear and amazement.

00:03:06.440 --> 00:03:08.240
And when we got
to know that we're

00:03:08.240 --> 00:03:10.640
going to talk about
psychology in a live webinar,

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:12.380
it give me a bit of
a terror, but then we

00:03:12.380 --> 00:03:14.210
got to know we have a lot
of people listening to us.

00:03:14.210 --> 00:03:15.860
That's a huge
piece of amazement.

00:03:15.860 --> 00:03:18.700
So we are in the state
of awe, pretty much.

00:03:18.700 --> 00:03:22.640
With that, let's talk
about what does it all mean

00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:25.190
for you, for your businesses.

00:03:25.190 --> 00:03:26.690
So this is emotional arithmetic.

00:03:26.690 --> 00:03:28.700
And it's extremely
important to understand

00:03:28.700 --> 00:03:31.365
that today's customer
feedbacks about product,

00:03:31.365 --> 00:03:34.650
about experiences, are
getting really nuanced.

00:03:34.650 --> 00:03:39.260
It's not just OK to understand
the sentiment of a customer's

00:03:39.260 --> 00:03:42.020
dialect or a customer's
written piece of text.

00:03:42.020 --> 00:03:44.660
We need to understand what
the customer is actually

00:03:44.660 --> 00:03:45.710
trying to convey.

00:03:45.710 --> 00:03:49.340
And that's when
we're going to get

00:03:49.340 --> 00:03:53.290
the juice of that comment
or the talk track,

00:03:53.290 --> 00:03:55.309
and be able to offer
them something really,

00:03:55.309 --> 00:03:56.100
really interesting.

00:03:56.100 --> 00:03:58.520
Let's look at a real sample.

00:03:58.520 --> 00:04:02.000
So this is from our demo that
we're going to do later today.

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:04.130
This is a product
review from one

00:04:04.130 --> 00:04:06.650
of our customers,
who's talking about one

00:04:06.650 --> 00:04:08.900
of the beauty products
that the person has bought

00:04:08.900 --> 00:04:11.960
from the e-commerce site.

00:04:11.960 --> 00:04:15.650
And the person talks about
that the product was great.

00:04:15.650 --> 00:04:17.420
The person got a
free sample of it.

00:04:17.420 --> 00:04:18.990
The experience was great.

00:04:18.990 --> 00:04:21.950
But then the pricing was not as
per the customer expectation.

00:04:21.950 --> 00:04:23.960
And at the very end,
the person is hopeful.

00:04:23.960 --> 00:04:29.330
And hopefully, the company is
going to correct the pricing

00:04:29.330 --> 00:04:32.120
and make it a really good
deal for the customer.

00:04:32.120 --> 00:04:34.530
ERIC SCHMIDT: And this
one feels like they're

00:04:34.530 --> 00:04:35.562
polar opposites, right?

00:04:35.562 --> 00:04:36.770
SAPTARSHI MUKHERJEE: Exactly.

00:04:36.770 --> 00:04:39.410
So if we just do a
simple sentiment analysis

00:04:39.410 --> 00:04:41.510
on this piece of
text, you will see

00:04:41.510 --> 00:04:44.180
there are a bunch of positive
sentiments flowing in.

00:04:44.180 --> 00:04:47.570
The product is great and the
company has done a great job.

00:04:47.570 --> 00:04:49.790
And then we have a bunch
of negative sentiments

00:04:49.790 --> 00:04:50.790
that are going in.

00:04:50.790 --> 00:04:52.580
So the simple sentiment
analysis is not

00:04:52.580 --> 00:04:54.080
going to give us great details.

00:04:54.080 --> 00:04:56.420
But if you go and apply
our emotional wheel,

00:04:56.420 --> 00:04:58.550
the Wheel of
Emotions, you will see

00:04:58.550 --> 00:05:01.360
that are nuanced emotions
playing throughout the text.

00:05:01.360 --> 00:05:03.530
There is a level
of anticipation.

00:05:03.530 --> 00:05:06.530
There's a level of joy and
trust, like this is great.

00:05:06.530 --> 00:05:09.120
I found the sample to
be working really well.

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:11.644
And then there are levels
of negative emotions

00:05:11.644 --> 00:05:13.810
throughout the text, like
the pricing was not right.

00:05:13.810 --> 00:05:16.090
There was a level of
anger and disgust.

00:05:16.090 --> 00:05:19.330
So if we apply the
Wheel of Emotion model

00:05:19.330 --> 00:05:21.890
here to understand how we
can delight this customer,

00:05:21.890 --> 00:05:25.270
if you see the person
has anticipation and joy.

00:05:25.270 --> 00:05:28.710
And if I merge these
two, I see a lot of hope.

00:05:28.710 --> 00:05:32.410
And there is a level
of anticipation

00:05:32.410 --> 00:05:33.400
that the customer has.

00:05:33.400 --> 00:05:36.730
And correcting the price
may convert this customer

00:05:36.730 --> 00:05:40.360
into a great ambassador
for the product.

00:05:40.360 --> 00:05:43.780
So with that, let's talk about
real-life examples of where

00:05:43.780 --> 00:05:46.150
we can apply the
emotional arithmetic,

00:05:46.150 --> 00:05:49.775
and understand emotion,
analyze the online retail.

00:05:49.775 --> 00:05:52.900
We're going to show the demo and
talk about how we apply there.

00:05:52.900 --> 00:05:55.780
But for example, gaming
analytics-- a lot of companies

00:05:55.780 --> 00:05:57.430
are providing gaming titles.

00:05:57.430 --> 00:06:00.220
The gamers are all the time very
vocal about their experience

00:06:00.220 --> 00:06:01.490
with these games.

00:06:01.490 --> 00:06:03.670
And if we can understand
their emotions,

00:06:03.670 --> 00:06:06.370
we'll be able to offer them
more personalized gaming

00:06:06.370 --> 00:06:07.660
experiences.

00:06:07.660 --> 00:06:10.210
And marketing overall
is trying to be

00:06:10.210 --> 00:06:12.050
more relevant to the
intended audience.

00:06:12.050 --> 00:06:15.400
So it has huge application
in the field of marketing

00:06:15.400 --> 00:06:17.980
analytics, try to making
sure that the campaigns

00:06:17.980 --> 00:06:22.270
and the content are as
for the audience's needs.

00:06:22.270 --> 00:06:26.830
Now, if you try to analyze
customer's emotions

00:06:26.830 --> 00:06:29.050
in real time, and they're
coming from millions

00:06:29.050 --> 00:06:32.032
of your customers, you
can't do it manually.

00:06:32.032 --> 00:06:32.740
ERIC SCHMIDT: No.

00:06:32.740 --> 00:06:33.656
Your example is great.

00:06:33.656 --> 00:06:35.290
You showed us one,
but how do we do

00:06:35.290 --> 00:06:37.570
this with millions and
millions of comments

00:06:37.570 --> 00:06:38.752
over millions of customers?

00:06:38.752 --> 00:06:40.210
SAPTARSHI MUKHERJEE:
Yeah, and then

00:06:40.210 --> 00:06:44.270
it pretty much becomes a big
data problem for all of us.

00:06:44.270 --> 00:06:47.600
Here is what Google Cloud has
to offer in the data analytics

00:06:47.600 --> 00:06:48.100
space.

00:06:48.100 --> 00:06:50.890
And we make it really simple for
our customers, because our data

00:06:50.890 --> 00:06:53.470
analytics platform
is serverless.

00:06:53.470 --> 00:06:55.360
So what it means is
basically, for you

00:06:55.360 --> 00:07:00.340
to build a emotional analytics
model, apply machine learning,

00:07:00.340 --> 00:07:03.190
understand human
emotions, and then apply

00:07:03.190 --> 00:07:05.710
our learning into a
product personalization,

00:07:05.710 --> 00:07:07.360
you can do this
pretty much end to end

00:07:07.360 --> 00:07:09.790
without thinking about any
infrastructure, capacity

00:07:09.790 --> 00:07:13.450
planning, load balancing--
nothing of that sort.

00:07:13.450 --> 00:07:15.970
Because anything else,
those infrastructural needs

00:07:15.970 --> 00:07:17.960
are taken care of
by the platform.

00:07:17.960 --> 00:07:19.660
And as an analyst,
you can just simply

00:07:19.660 --> 00:07:21.520
focus on doing data analytics.

00:07:21.520 --> 00:07:24.160
And we're going to talk
about all of these tools

00:07:24.160 --> 00:07:25.960
in the context of the demo.

00:07:25.960 --> 00:07:28.480
Now, the first thing in
the data analytics stack

00:07:28.480 --> 00:07:30.820
is the data management
platform, where

00:07:30.820 --> 00:07:33.430
you store all your
customer feedbacks,

00:07:33.430 --> 00:07:36.580
the emotions in real
time as they come in.

00:07:36.580 --> 00:07:40.040
And our offering in this
space is Google BigQuery.

00:07:40.040 --> 00:07:42.020
BigQuery is different.

00:07:42.020 --> 00:07:43.000
It is a data warehouse.

00:07:43.000 --> 00:07:44.416
It's an enterprise
data warehouse.

00:07:44.416 --> 00:07:47.230
But it was designed
for an ML-first world.

00:07:47.230 --> 00:07:49.810
If you look at the
evolution of data warehouses

00:07:49.810 --> 00:07:52.026
over the course of
the last many years,

00:07:52.026 --> 00:07:53.650
data warehouses were
initially designed

00:07:53.650 --> 00:07:57.130
for a BI-first,
reporting-first batch paradigm.

00:07:57.130 --> 00:07:59.200
And now, as companies
are increasingly

00:07:59.200 --> 00:08:02.260
investing in science
workflows and building machine

00:08:02.260 --> 00:08:05.027
learning models from their
most important data sets,

00:08:05.027 --> 00:08:07.360
the data warehouses need to
live up to that expectation.

00:08:07.360 --> 00:08:10.250
And that's what
exactly BigQuery does.

00:08:10.250 --> 00:08:15.280
So the first thing is,
BigQuery is massively scalable.

00:08:15.280 --> 00:08:18.400
So whether you analyze
your customer feedback

00:08:18.400 --> 00:08:21.640
from a few hundred customers
or millions of customers,

00:08:21.640 --> 00:08:25.540
the same system is going to
support your analysis needs.

00:08:25.540 --> 00:08:26.800
It is very simple to use.

00:08:26.800 --> 00:08:29.160
It supports ANSI SQL.

00:08:29.160 --> 00:08:32.480
You can write queries in the
language that you already know.

00:08:32.480 --> 00:08:34.659
And it supports
real-time data analysis.

00:08:34.659 --> 00:08:36.280
Like data warehouse,
we typically

00:08:36.280 --> 00:08:39.224
relate to the batch paradigm,
but BigQuery supports

00:08:39.224 --> 00:08:40.390
real-time streaming inserts.

00:08:40.390 --> 00:08:42.181
And we're going to show
that, how it works.

00:08:42.181 --> 00:08:44.039
And it's encrypted, durable.

00:08:44.039 --> 00:08:46.540
It kind of checks
all the boxes that

00:08:46.540 --> 00:08:49.060
are needed in a modern
data warehouse system.

00:08:49.060 --> 00:08:51.670
So with that, it's
almost time for the code.

00:08:51.670 --> 00:08:53.350
Let's make it real.

00:08:53.350 --> 00:08:54.900
ERIC SCHMIDT:
