00:09:00.070 --> 00:09:02.830
ERIC SCHMIDT: All right,
so as we get into the demo,

00:09:02.830 --> 00:09:05.350
we need to do some
pre-work, because you just

00:09:05.350 --> 00:09:09.310
can't go build an online
site or a data warehouse,

00:09:09.310 --> 00:09:11.590
unless you go and get
data, and you ingest data,

00:09:11.590 --> 00:09:13.640
and start doing some analysis.

00:09:13.640 --> 00:09:16.510
So the first thing that
I want to talk about

00:09:16.510 --> 00:09:19.120
is, if you wanted
to go do analysis,

00:09:19.120 --> 00:09:21.820
say emotional analysis
over comments and orders,

00:09:21.820 --> 00:09:24.460
et cetera, you're going
to have to go get data

00:09:24.460 --> 00:09:27.520
from various systems-- your CRM
system, billing, point of sale,

00:09:27.520 --> 00:09:28.270
et cetera.

00:09:28.270 --> 00:09:30.530
And you may use a myriad
of different tools.

00:09:30.530 --> 00:09:32.390
So we work with
lots of companies.

00:09:32.390 --> 00:09:34.510
And the average
rate right now is

00:09:34.510 --> 00:09:36.790
that most companies use
around 30 different tools

00:09:36.790 --> 00:09:40.840
to do ingestion, transformation,
analysis, et cetera.

00:09:40.840 --> 00:09:43.750
If your goal is to get a
holistic view of customers,

00:09:43.750 --> 00:09:45.610
you end up having
to use lots of tools

00:09:45.610 --> 00:09:49.040
over lots of different systems.

00:09:49.040 --> 00:09:52.060
One of the tools that
we used for this demo

00:09:52.060 --> 00:09:54.500
is Google Cloud Dataflow.

00:09:54.500 --> 00:09:59.620
What Dataflow enables you
to do is to point a job,

00:09:59.620 --> 00:10:03.500
a graph of instructions
at some data source,

00:10:03.500 --> 00:10:07.820
read from that source, provide
a set of transformations.

00:10:07.820 --> 00:10:09.454
Maybe you want to
read user comments,

00:10:09.454 --> 00:10:11.120
and you want to parse
out all the words,

00:10:11.120 --> 00:10:13.100
or you want to load
your line items

00:10:13.100 --> 00:10:16.490
and join that to some
catalog information.

00:10:16.490 --> 00:10:17.532
It doesn't really matter.

00:10:17.532 --> 00:10:19.406
You just go ahead and
point it at the source,

00:10:19.406 --> 00:10:21.490
run your transformations,
and then load your data

00:10:21.490 --> 00:10:24.830
to some destination
like BigQuery.

00:10:24.830 --> 00:10:27.740
The key here is that this
is completely serverless.

00:10:27.740 --> 00:10:31.580
And it is optimized for
embarrassingly parallelized

00:10:31.580 --> 00:10:34.880
operations, say like reading
100 million user comments

00:10:34.880 --> 00:10:36.860
and then loading
them into BigQuery.

00:10:36.860 --> 00:10:40.880
So I use Dataflow as my Step
One to go ahead and read data

00:10:40.880 --> 00:10:43.760
from all of my sources.

00:10:43.760 --> 00:10:45.890
With this, I was able
to then quickly get

00:10:45.890 --> 00:10:51.170
some high-level views of the
data using Data Studio, which

00:10:51.170 --> 00:10:53.750
is a serverless product.

00:10:53.750 --> 00:10:56.750
It's free, as well,
that you can use

00:10:56.750 --> 00:10:59.340
to build real-time,
interactive dashboard.

00:10:59.340 --> 00:11:02.000
So this dashboard is on
top of the demo, called

00:11:02.000 --> 00:11:03.050
"You Look Marvelous."

00:11:03.050 --> 00:11:05.570
We're going to show you
that here in a minute.

00:11:05.570 --> 00:11:09.500
This view is showing me some
high-level sales information--

00:11:09.500 --> 00:11:12.740
demographics, where are
my sales coming from,

00:11:12.740 --> 00:11:15.500
what's the difference
between, say, online sales

00:11:15.500 --> 00:11:18.332
and in-store sales.

00:11:18.332 --> 00:11:19.790
Here's another view
that we quickly

00:11:19.790 --> 00:11:23.370
built, which is how do
products purchase affinitize?

00:11:23.370 --> 00:11:25.220
So if people are
buying product A

00:11:25.220 --> 00:11:27.549
and they also buy a product
B, what's the likelihood

00:11:27.549 --> 00:11:29.090
that they would also
buy a product C,

00:11:29.090 --> 00:11:31.010
so I could build a
simple recommendation

00:11:31.010 --> 00:11:34.260
based on basket analysis.

00:11:34.260 --> 00:11:37.190
So I was quickly able
to go from, say, no data

00:11:37.190 --> 00:11:39.980
to ingesting data to
quickly visualizing it.

00:11:39.980 --> 00:11:41.480
And for this demo,
what I want to do

00:11:41.480 --> 00:11:43.350
is kind of flip the analysis.

00:11:43.350 --> 00:11:47.700
Instead of focusing on just the
customer view, or the product

00:11:47.700 --> 00:11:50.820
in terms of the
sales aspect of it,

00:11:50.820 --> 00:11:53.420
I want to understand why
did they buy a product.

00:11:53.420 --> 00:11:54.410
Was it effective?

00:11:54.410 --> 00:11:55.490
Would they buy it again?

00:11:55.490 --> 00:11:57.320
Because ultimately,
the more I can

00:11:57.320 --> 00:12:00.560
learn about the user's
behavior around the journey

00:12:00.560 --> 00:12:03.060
that they took to buy
a particular product,

00:12:03.060 --> 00:12:07.400
I could potentially influence a
new purchase, a new user that's

00:12:07.400 --> 00:12:09.440
coming to my site.

00:12:09.440 --> 00:12:12.200
So how do things work today?

00:12:12.200 --> 00:12:16.442
So Sap, you showed that example
of reading one user comment.

00:12:16.442 --> 00:12:18.650
And this is what we're going
to show you in the demo.

00:12:18.650 --> 00:12:21.320
How things work today is
you usually go into a site.

00:12:21.320 --> 00:12:25.130
You would type in a
free search, or maybe

00:12:25.130 --> 00:12:28.027
look at the taxonomy
of that site.

00:12:28.027 --> 00:12:29.360
You're trying to find a product.

00:12:29.360 --> 00:12:33.144
So maybe I'm trying to find
some cream for an acne problem.

00:12:33.144 --> 00:12:35.060
And I'm going to get a
bunch of search results

00:12:35.060 --> 00:12:36.800
that are ranked on
that site, typically

00:12:36.800 --> 00:12:40.640
by most popular, by
price, et cetera.

00:12:40.640 --> 00:12:42.590
And I start looking
at the ratings.

00:12:42.590 --> 00:12:43.880
And then I look at comments.

00:12:43.880 --> 00:12:46.080
And I keep doing this
process over and over again.

00:12:46.080 --> 00:12:47.930
And it might take
me 25, 30 minutes

00:12:47.930 --> 00:12:52.760
for me to decide whether or not
I'm going to buy this product.

00:12:52.760 --> 00:12:55.610
Now, let's play a game.

00:12:55.610 --> 00:12:57.940
Sap, you showed that one
that you previously scored.

00:12:57.940 --> 00:12:59.630
But in this case,
we don't have any

00:12:59.630 --> 00:13:03.470
of the dramatic struggle, the
two-time skin, definitely,

00:13:03.470 --> 00:13:04.830
et cetera, et cetera.

00:13:04.830 --> 00:13:06.800
This is where us,
as humans, we end up

00:13:06.800 --> 00:13:11.120
spending our time and also
emotional energy to figure out,

00:13:11.120 --> 00:13:12.800
is this comment a
good comment for me,

00:13:12.800 --> 00:13:16.500
in order to make a
purchasing decision?

00:13:16.500 --> 00:13:19.040
So what if we showed
you the results

00:13:19.040 --> 00:13:22.640
on that site that were ranked
by sentiment and effectiveness

00:13:22.640 --> 00:13:23.630
versus top selling?

00:13:23.630 --> 00:13:25.340
We can potentially
show you products

00:13:25.340 --> 00:13:28.040
that are more appropriate
for you higher in the rank.

00:13:28.040 --> 00:13:30.980
And then what if we showed you
comments once you were looking

00:13:30.980 --> 00:13:33.620
at a product that were more
appropriate for the product

00:13:33.620 --> 00:13:35.010
that you were looking at?

00:13:35.010 --> 00:13:37.040
So we could
ultimately shrink down

00:13:37.040 --> 00:13:39.080
the overall kind
of purchase time,

00:13:39.080 --> 00:13:41.840
and we could also provide
a more effective cycle

00:13:41.840 --> 00:13:45.440
to influence the best
possible purchase.

00:13:45.440 --> 00:13:48.600
So now that we
understand the context,

00:13:48.600 --> 00:13:49.600
let's get into the demo.

00:13:52.470 --> 00:13:55.100
The first thing I want to show
you is our "You Look Marvelous"

00:13:55.100 --> 00:13:56.090
site.

00:13:56.090 --> 00:14:00.644
This is a fake demo
built with fake products,

00:14:00.644 --> 00:14:02.810
but these products actually
exist in the real world.

00:14:02.810 --> 00:14:04.680
I just anonymised all of them.

00:14:04.680 --> 00:14:06.560
So here, I'm going to
go ahead and search

00:14:06.560 --> 00:14:12.170
for skincare, and treatments,
and blemish, and acne.

00:14:12.170 --> 00:14:14.600
So I'm going to go
ahead and do a search.

00:14:14.600 --> 00:14:18.050
And you'll see that the sort
order up here is called my

00:14:18.050 --> 00:14:19.580
You Look Marvelous score.

00:14:19.580 --> 00:14:21.230
Now typically,
this may be sorted

00:14:21.230 --> 00:14:23.180
by price or social popularity.

00:14:23.180 --> 00:14:26.150
But now, I have this You
Look Marvelous score.

00:14:26.150 --> 00:14:28.890
And you can see right here, this
product, the sale [INAUDIBLE]

00:14:28.890 --> 00:14:33.680
still max treatment has a
47% You Look Marvelous score.

00:14:33.680 --> 00:14:36.470
Now take a step
back, and oh, 47%--

00:14:36.470 --> 00:14:37.190
that sounds low.

00:14:37.190 --> 00:14:39.590
But it's the
second-ranked top product.

00:14:39.590 --> 00:14:42.739
So if I go ahead and
I click into this,

00:14:42.739 --> 00:14:44.780
it's going to go out and
retrieve all the product

00:14:44.780 --> 00:14:48.560
information for that particular
product, pull it back.

00:14:48.560 --> 00:14:49.440
Here's the product.

00:14:49.440 --> 00:14:51.080
This is what it
says it's good for.

00:14:51.080 --> 00:14:53.510
And I'll start
scrolling down, and you

00:14:53.510 --> 00:14:58.310
can see that this product has
an overall rating of 4.24.

00:14:58.310 --> 00:15:00.020
That sounds pretty good.

00:15:00.020 --> 00:15:02.464
It's got 105 five-star ratings.

00:15:02.464 --> 00:15:04.130
And you can kind of
see the distribution

00:15:04.130 --> 00:15:05.126
of the other ratings.

00:15:05.126 --> 00:15:07.250
So right here, if you were
just looking at ratings,

00:15:07.250 --> 00:15:08.510
you'd probably be influenced.

00:15:08.510 --> 00:15:09.770
Hey, this feels pretty good.

00:15:09.770 --> 00:15:12.020
This may be a good
product for me.

00:15:12.020 --> 00:15:14.230
But what we've also
done on the You

00:15:14.230 --> 00:15:17.990
Look Marvelous site is we've
taken all of the underlying

00:15:17.990 --> 00:15:23.840
comment information, and ran it
through our emotional analysis

00:15:23.840 --> 00:15:26.120
workflow that Sap
was talking about.

00:15:26.120 --> 00:15:28.844
And now you can see the
distribution of emotions.

00:15:28.844 --> 00:15:30.260
So you can start
making decisions.

00:15:30.260 --> 00:15:32.489
Well, there's a lot of
positivity around this.

00:15:32.489 --> 00:15:33.530
A lot of people loved it.

00:15:33.530 --> 00:15:35.090
A lot of people are--

00:15:35.090 --> 00:15:37.280
say 3% are showing anticipation.

00:15:37.280 --> 00:15:40.320
But there's also a decent
amount of negativity.

00:15:40.320 --> 00:15:42.531
And in the real world,
nothing's going to be perfect.

00:15:42.531 --> 00:15:44.030
You're always going
to have products

00:15:44.030 --> 00:15:45.950
that have some type
of distribution

00:15:45.950 --> 00:15:50.090
between positive and negative,
and then various aspects

00:15:50.090 --> 00:15:52.370
of emotional rating.

00:15:52.370 --> 00:15:55.220
And the last thing to show
you here is the comments.

00:15:55.220 --> 00:15:57.860
So these first
three comments-- we

00:15:57.860 --> 00:16:03.770
selected them based on
some psychological knobs

00:16:03.770 --> 00:16:08.770
that we built to determine what
comments are the most positive,

00:16:08.770 --> 00:16:12.170
and are also the most
information dense.

00:16:12.170 --> 00:16:14.660
So as a potential
purchaser, this

00:16:14.660 --> 00:16:16.315
is where we spend a lot of time.

00:16:16.315 --> 00:16:18.440
We're reading those comments,
trying to figure out,

00:16:18.440 --> 00:16:19.919
is this the best comment for me?

00:16:19.919 --> 00:16:21.710
What happens if you
didn't have to do that?

00:16:21.710 --> 00:16:24.320
On the You Look Marvelous
site, you don't.

00:16:24.320 --> 00:16:27.680
These comments are the comments
that are the most appropriate

00:16:27.680 --> 00:16:32.090
from an emotional aspect related
to the products' reported

00:16:32.090 --> 00:16:34.010
goals.

00:16:34.010 --> 00:16:37.010
As well as the comments that
have the most information

00:16:37.010 --> 00:16:38.540
density inside of them.

00:16:38.540 --> 00:16:39.930
So kind of keep that in mind.

00:16:39.930 --> 00:16:42.620
So this is kind of how we built
this You Look Marvelous score,

00:16:42.620 --> 00:16:47.310
and how we tried to improve the
overall shopping experience.

00:16:47.310 --> 00:16:49.880
Let's look at how we did that.

00:16:49.880 --> 00:16:53.990
So I talked a little bit about
getting data into BigQuery

00:16:53.990 --> 00:16:56.060
using Google Cloud Dataflow.

00:16:56.060 --> 00:16:57.800
I'm going to go ahead
and run this query.

00:16:57.800 --> 00:17:02.210
You can see these are all my
tables over here in my You

00:17:02.210 --> 00:17:04.369
Look Marvelous data warehouse.

00:17:04.369 --> 00:17:09.740
So I have comments, line items,
locations, orders, et cetera.

00:17:09.740 --> 00:17:11.690
And this query that
I'm writing right now

00:17:11.690 --> 00:17:16.130
is looking at a few
gigs worth of data.

00:17:16.130 --> 00:17:18.500
So it took just
under 15 seconds,

00:17:18.500 --> 00:17:21.810
and I scanned one and
a half gigs of data.

00:17:21.810 --> 00:17:27.109
What this query did was,
it's a relatively big query--

00:17:27.109 --> 00:17:28.550
no pun intended.

00:17:28.550 --> 00:17:31.850
I'm taking all of my orders,
joining them to all the line

00:17:31.850 --> 00:17:34.490
items, and then all the line
items to all the product

00:17:34.490 --> 00:17:37.310
information, and
then all the comments

00:17:37.310 --> 00:17:40.310
for all those products,
so three different

00:17:40.310 --> 00:17:43.910
joins across millions
and millions of rows.

00:17:43.910 --> 00:17:46.430
And I'm trying to calculate
the top 100 selling

00:17:46.430 --> 00:17:50.360
products based on sales, which
is something people normally

00:17:50.360 --> 00:17:51.220
think about.

00:17:51.220 --> 00:17:53.360
Hey, what are my top
100 selling products?

00:17:53.360 --> 00:17:54.110
SAPTARSHI MUKHERJEE:
Are you making money?

00:17:54.110 --> 00:17:54.840
ERIC SCHMIDT: Am I making money?

00:17:54.840 --> 00:17:56.390
Yeah, what products
are making money?

00:17:56.390 --> 00:17:58.700
But what I wanted
to know was, I want

00:17:58.700 --> 00:18:00.800
to rank those top
100 products also

00:18:00.800 --> 00:18:04.697
based on the average word
count in the comments.

00:18:04.697 --> 00:18:06.530
So I have millions and
millions of comments.

00:18:06.530 --> 00:18:09.110
I want to know the average word
count for my top 100 selling

00:18:09.110 --> 00:18:10.140
products.

00:18:10.140 --> 00:18:12.680
So you can see the product
ID starting with 417

00:18:12.680 --> 00:18:16.170
has an average
word count of 688.

00:18:16.170 --> 00:18:19.430
And then if I look at my
last product in the list,

00:18:19.430 --> 00:18:23.330
starting with 523, it has an
average word count of 182.

00:18:23.330 --> 00:18:25.159
So it's a pretty big spread.

00:18:25.159 --> 00:18:27.200
And you'll see why this
is important in a minute.

00:18:27.200 --> 00:18:31.010
The point here is, once you
have your data into BigQuery,

00:18:31.010 --> 00:18:35.100
you can express any type
of query that you want.

00:18:35.100 --> 00:18:37.550
And you don't have to worry
about indexing, partitioning,

00:18:37.550 --> 00:18:38.050
et cetera.

00:18:38.050 --> 00:18:42.770
You literally load data,
write query, period.

00:18:42.770 --> 00:18:45.680
So now that I've showed
you how to build a BigQuery

00:18:45.680 --> 00:18:49.160
inside of BigQuery, let's
go through a data analysis

00:18:49.160 --> 00:18:50.921
and data science
journey to build

00:18:50.921 --> 00:18:52.170
that You Look Marvelous score.

00:18:55.480 --> 00:18:59.180
I'm going to flip over to
another serverless product.

00:18:59.180 --> 00:19:01.440
This is Google Cloud Datalab.

00:19:01.440 --> 00:19:05.960
It is, in essence, an
optimized IPython environment

00:19:05.960 --> 00:19:08.870
that helps you interact
with Google Cloud resources

00:19:08.870 --> 00:19:11.990
as well as any other resource
that you would use inside

00:19:11.990 --> 00:19:13.670
of IPython.

00:19:13.670 --> 00:19:17.810
So here, this
notebook is focused

00:19:17.810 --> 00:19:21.587
on building that You
Look Marvelous score.

00:19:21.587 --> 00:19:23.920
SAPTARSHI MUKHERJEE: So are
you going to do that emotion

00:19:23.920 --> 00:19:25.010
arithmetic here?

00:19:25.010 --> 00:19:25.760
ERIC SCHMIDT: Yes.

00:19:25.760 --> 00:19:27.980
We're going to go from
basically raw product view

00:19:27.980 --> 00:19:30.320
down to what is that score?

00:19:30.320 --> 00:19:34.580
And how do you figure out
what comments to filter?

00:19:34.580 --> 00:19:37.130
The first thing to look
at inside of Datalab

00:19:37.130 --> 00:19:38.840
is we have these
magic functions.

00:19:38.840 --> 00:19:42.800
You see this SQL and
then module products.

00:19:42.800 --> 00:19:45.380
Here, I'm defining
a simple query.

00:19:45.380 --> 00:19:47.010
And whenever I
run this query, it

00:19:47.010 --> 00:19:49.520
will go out, push down
the query to BigQuery,

00:19:49.520 --> 00:19:51.110
and return those results.

00:19:51.110 --> 00:19:53.660
And I can easily
take SQL Results

00:19:53.660 --> 00:19:57.230
and convert them into pandas
DataFrames, which is the area

00:19:57.230 --> 00:19:59.330
that I'm more comfortable with.

00:19:59.330 --> 00:20:00.200
I love SQL.

00:20:00.200 --> 00:20:03.380
I love writing queries inside
of Big Query, et cetera.

00:20:03.380 --> 00:20:05.660
I want to get this data
inside of pandas DataFrames

00:20:05.660 --> 00:20:08.720
so I can quickly do
things like basic

00:20:08.720 --> 00:20:12.750
looking at descriptive
analysis on top of the data.

00:20:12.750 --> 00:20:14.090
So I went out.

00:20:14.090 --> 00:20:16.820
I queried all of my products.

00:20:16.820 --> 00:20:20.270
And I want to look at their
average star rating, the thing

00:20:20.270 --> 00:20:22.310
that we were influenced before.

00:20:22.310 --> 00:20:24.800
So here is all the
products in my catalog.

00:20:24.800 --> 00:20:30.110
And you see things that are
tightly clustered in the--

00:20:30.110 --> 00:20:33.290
at the 50th percentile,
we're about 4.2,

00:20:33.290 --> 00:20:35.960
70th percentile about 4.4.

00:20:35.960 --> 00:20:38.870
Now, whenever I was doing the
analysis-- and by the way,

00:20:38.870 --> 00:20:40.010
this data is real--

00:20:40.010 --> 00:20:40.820
this struck me.

00:20:40.820 --> 00:20:45.380
I was, like, oh, I sell products
that people like overall.

00:20:45.380 --> 00:20:47.270
You see it's very, very
tightly distributed.

00:20:47.270 --> 00:20:49.561
If I didn't care about the
products that I was selling,

00:20:49.561 --> 00:20:51.560
you'd probably see
some outliers you know

00:20:51.560 --> 00:20:53.540
further down in the ratings.

00:20:53.540 --> 00:20:55.760
As a customer, if
I was using ratings

00:20:55.760 --> 00:20:59.710
as a mechanism to disambiguate
one product from another,

00:20:59.710 --> 00:21:00.210
this is it.

00:21:00.210 --> 00:21:01.700
You're headed down a bad path.

00:21:01.700 --> 00:21:02.695
SAPTARSHI MUKHERJEE:
There's too many choices.

00:21:02.695 --> 00:21:03.590
ERIC SCHMIDT: There's
too many choices, right.

00:21:03.590 --> 00:21:05.490
They all look the same.

00:21:05.490 --> 00:21:09.290
So the next thing I did was I
took all of my user comments

00:21:09.290 --> 00:21:12.230
for those products, and I ran
them through the Google Cloud

00:21:12.230 --> 00:21:14.180
Natural Language API.

00:21:14.180 --> 00:21:16.780
And I'll flip over
to that real quickly.

00:21:16.780 --> 00:21:17.924
Here's the product page.

00:21:17.924 --> 00:21:20.090
And you can go ahead and
test this with any comments

00:21:20.090 --> 00:21:21.020
that you want.

00:21:21.020 --> 00:21:23.810
I pasted in a comment
and I hit Analyze.

00:21:23.810 --> 00:21:26.870
And you can see that the
Google Cloud Natural Language

00:21:26.870 --> 00:21:30.194
API will provide you sentiment
at the document level

00:21:30.194 --> 00:21:31.610
as well as at the
sentiment level.

00:21:31.610 --> 00:21:34.068
So you can see that distribution
that Sap was talking about

00:21:34.068 --> 00:21:35.090
before.

00:21:35.090 --> 00:21:38.300
It also picks out the entities,
like this a skin cream.

00:21:38.300 --> 00:21:41.290
Here is the sentiment
against that entity.

00:21:41.290 --> 00:21:44.120
And it also provides
you the syntax tree,

00:21:44.120 --> 00:21:48.152
so if you wanted to do some
brute-force lexical processing

00:21:48.152 --> 00:21:49.610
on this, you could
say, hey, I want

00:21:49.610 --> 00:21:51.740
to look at the top
number of verbs,

00:21:51.740 --> 00:21:53.180
or I want to look
at noun phrases,

00:21:53.180 --> 00:21:56.990
or I want to look at the
relationships of one syntax

00:21:56.990 --> 00:21:58.790
element from another.

00:21:58.790 --> 00:22:01.710
So you get all of this
information back from the Cloud

00:22:01.710 --> 00:22:04.100
Natural Language API.

00:22:04.100 --> 00:22:07.460
And I can use Dataflow to inject
them into my data warehouse.

00:22:07.460 --> 00:22:11.660
So now, instead of just
looking at scores, ratings,

00:22:11.660 --> 00:22:15.740
I'm now looking at sentiment, so
I run a slightly bigger query.

00:22:15.740 --> 00:22:19.670
And this is what the
distribution of sentiment

00:22:19.670 --> 00:22:22.070
looks like for those products.

00:22:22.070 --> 00:22:24.580
And again, whenever I was doing
this, I was, like, oh, well,

00:22:24.580 --> 00:22:26.580
in this case, humans are
doing the right things.

00:22:26.580 --> 00:22:29.150
So I know I have
very tight clustering

00:22:29.150 --> 00:22:31.500
that's in the positive
range, so around, say,

00:22:31.500 --> 00:22:35.840
everything's 50th percentile is
around 0.9 or 1, which is good.

00:22:35.840 --> 00:22:39.410
Overall, there is strong
positive sentiment

00:22:39.410 --> 00:22:41.040
for the products
that I'm selling.

00:22:41.040 --> 00:22:43.550
So if we go ahead
and pivot this,

00:22:43.550 --> 00:22:46.070
if you were the owner
of this company,

00:22:46.070 --> 00:22:48.830
you're, like, hey, this graph
makes me feel good-- three,

00:22:48.830 --> 00:22:52.700
four, five stars
up until the right.

00:22:52.700 --> 00:22:53.840
I don't sell any garbage.

00:22:53.840 --> 00:22:56.540
I don't have any star
ratings in one and two.

00:22:56.540 --> 00:22:59.390
But as a customer, you're not
buying the entire catalog.

00:22:59.390 --> 00:23:01.100
You're trying to
buy one product.

00:23:01.100 --> 00:23:03.370
So that product that
I just showed you,

00:23:03.370 --> 00:23:07.990
on the You Look Marvelous site,
is this product right here.

00:23:07.990 --> 00:23:10.470
So how do you start to
disambiguate between, say,

00:23:10.470 --> 00:23:12.010
three, four, and
five-star ratings

00:23:12.010 --> 00:23:14.042
that the averages in
standard deviation,

00:23:14.042 --> 00:23:16.000
especially between four
and five-star ratings--

00:23:16.000 --> 00:23:17.110
they're almost the same.

00:23:17.110 --> 00:23:19.170
So again, this data
isn't really going

00:23:19.170 --> 00:23:21.810
to help you as a customer.

00:23:21.810 --> 00:23:24.660
So the next thing I did
is, I needed more data.

00:23:24.660 --> 00:23:29.580
And I went and I used an open
source library called Sirocco

00:23:29.580 --> 00:23:33.600
that helps you calculate scores
for the Wheel of Emotions

00:23:33.600 --> 00:23:35.580
that Sap was talking about.

00:23:35.580 --> 00:23:38.610
So I ran all of my
comments through Sirocco,

00:23:38.610 --> 00:23:42.075
and now I have a much
wider data set to look at.

00:23:42.075 --> 00:23:44.460
In essence, I can
write a bigger query.

00:23:44.460 --> 00:23:47.340
You see, now I am selecting
all these emotions.

00:23:47.340 --> 00:23:50.730
And I'm going to go ahead
and use a slightly different

00:23:50.730 --> 00:23:51.640
visual technique.

00:23:51.640 --> 00:23:53.160
Here, I just have
a heat map, where

00:23:53.160 --> 00:23:55.380
I have the ratings
on the vertical,

00:23:55.380 --> 00:23:58.440
and I have the emotions
on the horizontal.

00:23:58.440 --> 00:24:00.690
And what you see here,
your eye kind of naturally

00:24:00.690 --> 00:24:03.290
gravitates towards
up and to the right.

00:24:03.290 --> 00:24:05.220
Here's where the
most kind of density

00:24:05.220 --> 00:24:07.200
is around those raw scores.

00:24:07.200 --> 00:24:10.290
So I see a lot of joy,
acceptance, surprise.

00:24:10.290 --> 00:24:11.050
This is good.

00:24:11.050 --> 00:24:12.730
This is what you'd expect.

00:24:12.730 --> 00:24:15.100
But as I start looking left,
I'm, like, wait a minute.

00:24:15.100 --> 00:24:19.781
Why is there fear and
some sadness showing up

00:24:19.781 --> 00:24:21.030
in this product, even though--

00:24:21.030 --> 00:24:21.900
SAPTARSHI MUKHERJEE:
It was highly rated.

00:24:21.900 --> 00:24:23.850
ERIC SCHMIDT: Yeah,
it was highly rated.

00:24:23.850 --> 00:24:26.490
And whenever I did this
journey, I was, like, ha ha.

00:24:26.490 --> 00:24:30.130
I can go dumpster dive
on even more data,

00:24:30.130 --> 00:24:32.130
but I had to take a step
back, saying, well, I'm

00:24:32.130 --> 00:24:36.210
trying to build a better ranking
system for these products.

00:24:36.210 --> 00:24:37.387
Now, I did do that analysis.

00:24:37.387 --> 00:24:39.926
So yes, people were
fearful that they're not

00:24:39.926 --> 00:24:41.550
going to be able to
afford the product.

00:24:41.550 --> 00:24:43.742
Because they got the
product as a sample.

00:24:43.742 --> 00:24:45.450
So they got it from
a friend as a sample.

00:24:45.450 --> 00:24:46.080
It was free.

00:24:46.080 --> 00:24:47.580
And then it's working for them.

00:24:47.580 --> 00:24:51.090
But now they're fearful that
they may not be able to buy it.

00:24:51.090 --> 00:24:54.660
Or they're saddened because
the product doesn't necessarily

00:24:54.660 --> 00:24:56.402
provide--

00:24:56.402 --> 00:24:58.110
the packaging doesn't
provide the ability

00:24:58.110 --> 00:25:00.030
to get all the
product out of it.

00:25:00.030 --> 00:25:02.550
There are all types
of reasons why

00:25:02.550 --> 00:25:04.500
the emotion would pop up here.

00:25:04.500 --> 00:25:07.560
Now, with this
information, I now

00:25:07.560 --> 00:25:11.220
have the basis to start
building a better sort order

00:25:11.220 --> 00:25:13.440
for my comments and products.

00:25:13.440 --> 00:25:16.120
Just for the heck of it,
I ran a much bigger query.

00:25:16.120 --> 00:25:20.100
So this is Sirocco, the emotion
analysis across all products,

00:25:20.100 --> 00:25:21.510
across all comments.

00:25:21.510 --> 00:25:25.170
So you can see overall, up
in the top right quadrant,

00:25:25.170 --> 00:25:26.940
people like my products.

00:25:26.940 --> 00:25:29.270
But there's a lot of fear,
and guilt, and sadness, et

00:25:29.270 --> 00:25:31.830
cetera, that I may want to go
dispatch, to see my product

00:25:31.830 --> 00:25:33.600
quality folks, say,
hey, go figure out

00:25:33.600 --> 00:25:36.060
what's going on here.

00:25:36.060 --> 00:25:40.012
Now, once I had this data, I
went back to the customers.

00:25:40.012 --> 00:25:41.970
Because we actually did
this with the customers

00:25:41.970 --> 00:25:42.430
we were working with.

00:25:42.430 --> 00:25:44.138
I was, like, what
would you want to show?

00:25:44.138 --> 00:25:46.380
If you knew the emotional
makeup of these comments,

00:25:46.380 --> 00:25:47.546
what would you want to show?

00:25:47.546 --> 00:25:50.310
And they said, we want to
show customers comments that

00:25:50.310 --> 00:25:54.570
are the most objective, in
essence, the most information

00:25:54.570 --> 00:25:56.700
dense related to the product.

00:25:56.700 --> 00:25:58.000
So we did some research.

00:25:58.000 --> 00:26:00.090
And it turns out there's
an inverse relationship

00:26:00.090 --> 00:26:04.150
between objectivity and emotion.

00:26:04.150 --> 00:26:05.760
So I tried to prove that.

00:26:05.760 --> 00:26:08.740
So I went and wrote a fairly
complex query in BigQuery.

00:26:08.740 --> 00:26:11.380
So I'm basically pushing
down this analysis.

00:26:11.380 --> 00:26:14.190
And what I wanted to
do was find the top two

00:26:14.190 --> 00:26:19.230
comments per star rating
that were the most emotional.

00:26:19.230 --> 00:26:20.820
So here's the answer to that.

00:26:20.820 --> 00:26:21.810
I love, love, love.

00:26:21.810 --> 00:26:24.060
I can't keep going on how
much I love this product.

00:26:24.060 --> 00:26:25.860
I love this gel, et
cetera, et cetera.

00:26:25.860 --> 00:26:27.832
This is a highly
emotional comment.

00:26:27.832 --> 00:26:29.790
And you start reading
it, and you're not really

00:26:29.790 --> 00:26:34.530
getting any objective
information about the product.

00:26:34.530 --> 00:26:36.754
And you can see how
verbose these are.

00:26:36.754 --> 00:26:38.670
In essence, a lot of
these are a waste of time

00:26:38.670 --> 00:26:41.070
if you're going to show
them to the end user.

00:26:41.070 --> 00:26:42.660
So we proved that.

00:26:42.660 --> 00:26:45.690
And then if we want
to get to objectivity,

00:26:45.690 --> 00:26:48.840
what would this look like if
I pulled out all the emotion?

00:26:48.840 --> 00:26:52.860
So I basically take that
same query and I reverse it.

00:26:52.860 --> 00:26:55.320
Now, these are the
most objective comments

00:26:55.320 --> 00:26:56.940
in five, four, threes, two, one.

00:26:56.940 --> 00:27:00.450
So "I can't say enough about how
much I love this black line,"

00:27:00.450 --> 00:27:02.520
or "hydrates without
a greasy feeling,

00:27:02.520 --> 00:27:05.040
absorbs in the skin quickly,
and is lightweight."

00:27:05.040 --> 00:27:07.380
This is a great comment.

00:27:07.380 --> 00:27:11.940
It's information rich, and
it talks to the properties

00:27:11.940 --> 00:27:13.710
of the product.

00:27:13.710 --> 00:27:17.400
So with this, I now have a
way to go ahead and start

00:27:17.400 --> 00:27:20.760
determining what
comments I want to show

00:27:20.760 --> 00:27:21.900
for a particular product.

00:27:24.900 --> 00:27:27.720
And the idea here
was, whenever we're

00:27:27.720 --> 00:27:30.120
working with our
customers, they said, well,

00:27:30.120 --> 00:27:33.010
we just don't want to show
all positive comments.

00:27:33.010 --> 00:27:35.790
The first one we show is
going to be the most positive

00:27:35.790 --> 00:27:37.320
and the most objective.

00:27:37.320 --> 00:27:41.040
The second one
would be slightly--

00:27:41.040 --> 00:27:43.980
it would be the most positive
with a little bit of emotion,

00:27:43.980 --> 00:27:47.400
so you have a sense of yeah,
this isn't just a robot

00:27:47.400 --> 00:27:48.780
talking about something.

00:27:48.780 --> 00:27:50.880
And then if I want to
show negative emotion,

00:27:50.880 --> 00:27:53.730
I want to show emotion around
things that I can control.

00:27:53.730 --> 00:27:57.000
I can't control if people
are angry about packaging,

00:27:57.000 --> 00:27:58.650
because I don't
control the packaging.

00:27:58.650 --> 00:28:02.340
But I could help them
if their emotion, say,

00:28:02.340 --> 00:28:04.310
were coming from fear,
maybe about pricing

00:28:04.310 --> 00:28:05.760
or something like that.

00:28:05.760 --> 00:28:08.450
So once you have
that data, you can

00:28:08.450 --> 00:28:12.440
start playing with the knobs
in terms of what you actually

00:28:12.440 --> 00:28:14.540
want to show.

00:28:14.540 --> 00:28:15.729
So now we have this score.

00:28:15.729 --> 00:28:16.770
We have this basis score.

00:28:16.770 --> 00:28:20.690
And you can use-- you can
weight it however you want.

00:28:20.690 --> 00:28:22.570
But we didn't stop there.

00:28:22.570 --> 00:28:25.400
The next thing that we
wanted to look at was,

00:28:25.400 --> 00:28:28.940
could we determine some
additional information

00:28:28.940 --> 00:28:32.840
about a commenter, as
well as a user on a site,

00:28:32.840 --> 00:28:35.000
to potentially match
them with comments

00:28:35.000 --> 00:28:39.620
that were more appropriate
for them demographically?

00:28:39.620 --> 00:28:42.080
So the question would be,
what could we look at?

00:28:42.080 --> 00:28:47.480
What cities did they
register from, et cetera?

00:28:47.480 --> 00:28:50.120
What types of
products did we buy?

00:28:50.120 --> 00:28:52.800
What we wanted to
look at was age range.

00:28:52.800 --> 00:28:57.230
So in the cosmetics space,
cosmetics across age

00:28:57.230 --> 00:29:00.400
ranges, they behave differently.

00:29:00.400 --> 00:29:02.620
And people talk about
them differently.

00:29:02.620 --> 00:29:05.270
And in our data set,
we had 20% of our users

00:29:05.270 --> 00:29:09.980
who had self-identified into
an age range, basically 18

00:29:09.980 --> 00:29:13.100
to 26, 27 to 35, et cetera.

00:29:13.100 --> 00:29:16.280
So the question was,
could we analyze

00:29:16.280 --> 00:29:18.830
that data to see if there
was a difference between

00:29:18.830 --> 00:29:23.270
how, say, a baby boomer
communicates versus how

00:29:23.270 --> 00:29:25.430
a millennial communicates?

00:29:25.430 --> 00:29:27.990
And if you could see
those differences,

00:29:27.990 --> 00:29:30.660
then we could build
a second knob to say,

00:29:30.660 --> 00:29:33.380
well, we only want to show
baby boomers comments from baby

00:29:33.380 --> 00:29:36.350
boomers, and we only want
to show millennials comments

00:29:36.350 --> 00:29:37.640
from millennials.

00:29:37.640 --> 00:29:40.750
So how do we do that?

00:29:40.750 --> 00:29:44.780
I'm going to flip over to
another Datalab notebook.

00:29:44.780 --> 00:29:48.290
This is the notebook that we
built to do age classification.

00:29:48.290 --> 00:29:51.560
So using very
similar techniques,

00:29:51.560 --> 00:29:56.480
I started my journey by
pulling in a raw data set

00:29:56.480 --> 00:29:59.840
from BigQuery, in essence, all
the comments and the buckets

00:29:59.840 --> 00:30:01.040
that users were in.

00:30:01.040 --> 00:30:04.900
So I only had 20% of my
users had classified age

00:30:04.900 --> 00:30:05.920
or had talked about age.

00:30:05.920 --> 00:30:08.260
So I'm trying to basically
project and classify

00:30:08.260 --> 00:30:10.670
to the 80% of population.

00:30:10.670 --> 00:30:15.020
Here, I'm using Keras sitting
on top of TensorFlow as well

00:30:15.020 --> 00:30:16.280
as BigQuery.

00:30:16.280 --> 00:30:19.220
And I'm going to go
ahead and move down

00:30:19.220 --> 00:30:24.500
to the bulk of the
model that I built.

00:30:24.500 --> 00:30:27.230
The idea here is that
for every comment,

00:30:27.230 --> 00:30:31.190
I have N number of words, and
I'm using an embedding model.

00:30:31.190 --> 00:30:35.250
I'm basically saying
for the first 100 words

00:30:35.250 --> 00:30:40.150
in that comment, I want to
identify the most closest words

00:30:40.150 --> 00:30:40.650
to them.

00:30:40.650 --> 00:30:41.930
So it's a technique
called embedding.

00:30:41.930 --> 00:30:44.221
So Sap, if you use the word,
"like" and I use the word,

00:30:44.221 --> 00:30:46.200
"love," those words
are fairly close.

00:30:46.200 --> 00:30:48.890
If you use the word, "hot"
and I use the word, "cold,"

00:30:48.890 --> 00:30:50.420
those words are
fairly far apart.

00:30:50.420 --> 00:30:53.870
So the idea here is that
if we can cluster comments

00:30:53.870 --> 00:30:56.360
based on the distance
between those words,

00:30:56.360 --> 00:30:57.860
we could start
seeing, yeah, there's

00:30:57.860 --> 00:31:00.260
a difference between
how millennials

00:31:00.260 --> 00:31:02.010
and boomers communicate.

00:31:02.010 --> 00:31:09.290
So I go ahead and I
run my training data

00:31:09.290 --> 00:31:10.650
to train this model.

00:31:10.650 --> 00:31:13.025
And there are some interesting
things that pop out of it.

00:31:14.960 --> 00:31:16.610
You can see here
that I'm going ahead,

00:31:16.610 --> 00:31:18.860
and I'm calling out
to BigQuery, and I

00:31:18.860 --> 00:31:21.410
pulled back 21,000 comments.

00:31:21.410 --> 00:31:24.490
So I'm going to train
on 16,000 of them.

00:31:24.490 --> 00:31:28.260
I try to distribute the
groups between Group 1

00:31:28.260 --> 00:31:29.810
and Group 2,
millennials and boomers.

00:31:29.810 --> 00:31:32.630
I try to keep the
distribution even so I'm not

00:31:32.630 --> 00:31:35.120
creating some type of bias.

00:31:35.120 --> 00:31:40.430
And for those
16,000-some comments,

00:31:40.430 --> 00:31:44.694
it found 14,302 unique
words, which is good.

00:31:44.694 --> 00:31:46.610
This is how I'm going
to create my embeddings.

00:31:46.610 --> 00:31:49.520
One of the problems I ran into
is that humans, unfortunately,

00:31:49.520 --> 00:31:52.770
don't spell correctly
100% of the time.

00:31:52.770 --> 00:31:56.840
So out of the 14,000
words, 3,200 of these words

00:31:56.840 --> 00:31:59.240
had null embeddings,
so that people

00:31:59.240 --> 00:32:02.570
spelled "moisturizer"
incorrectly, or "breakout,"

00:32:02.570 --> 00:32:03.600
et cetera.

00:32:03.600 --> 00:32:07.910
So basically, I've kind of
taken the fidelity of the data

00:32:07.910 --> 00:32:09.290
that I had.

00:32:09.290 --> 00:32:13.082
I have higher fidelity, but
I have less data to analyze.

00:32:13.082 --> 00:32:14.790
So I was kind of
concerned at this point.

00:32:14.790 --> 00:32:17.570
I'm like, oh, no,
humans can't spell.

00:32:17.570 --> 00:32:18.230
That's OK.

00:32:18.230 --> 00:32:19.140
I kept going.

00:32:19.140 --> 00:32:21.080
I kept training.

00:32:21.080 --> 00:32:25.200
And with the data, even with
the quality issues I had,

00:32:25.200 --> 00:32:28.867
within 62%, I can accurately
predict whether or not

00:32:28.867 --> 00:32:30.200
you're a millennial or a boomer.

00:32:30.200 --> 00:32:31.560
So here's a good example.

00:32:31.560 --> 00:32:33.980
So my predicted label was
1, which is a millennial.

00:32:33.980 --> 00:32:35.330
The actual label is 1.

00:32:35.330 --> 00:32:37.220
This says, "I'm a
teenager, so I just

00:32:37.220 --> 00:32:39.840
started breaking out after I
washed my face, et cetera."

00:32:39.840 --> 00:32:42.350
Now in this case, "teenager"
is a really, really good hint.

00:32:42.350 --> 00:32:44.750
If you see, "I'm a
teenager," "teen," et cetera,

00:32:44.750 --> 00:32:49.220
you know the clustering is going
to be way, way more efficient.

00:32:49.220 --> 00:32:52.330
This one, boomer to boomer,
you kind of read it,

00:32:52.330 --> 00:32:54.080
and you're, like, well,
obviously, there's

00:32:54.080 --> 00:32:56.090
some type of
information, some type

00:32:56.090 --> 00:32:59.820
of pattern within that text that
classified them as a boomer.

00:32:59.820 --> 00:33:01.820
And the great part is
that you don't necessarily

00:33:01.820 --> 00:33:04.230
have to go and
decompose why it is.

00:33:04.230 --> 00:33:06.210
You just know that you
accurately predicted.

00:33:06.210 --> 00:33:08.580
And I think with some
more tuning techniques,

00:33:08.580 --> 00:33:11.580
and cleaning up these
bad embeddings up here,

00:33:11.580 --> 00:33:15.160
I could probably get this to
about 70%, which is awesome.

00:33:15.160 --> 00:33:16.830
So now I have the
ability to tune a knob

00:33:16.830 --> 00:33:19.500
and say whether or not you're
a millennial or a boomer.

00:33:19.500 --> 00:33:22.530
And I also have knobs
to tune whether or not

00:33:22.530 --> 00:33:26.580
you want to see emotional
or non-emotional, objective

00:33:26.580 --> 00:33:29.040
or non-objective comments.

00:33:29.040 --> 00:33:34.980
So using those two techniques,
if you come back to the site,

00:33:34.980 --> 00:33:37.130
those two techniques
are ultimately

00:33:37.130 --> 00:33:40.860
what is driving what is
rendered on this site.

00:33:40.860 --> 00:33:43.140
On the back end, what we
don't show in this demo,

00:33:43.140 --> 00:33:46.380
there's another
seminar that has been

00:33:46.380 --> 00:33:48.870
done on the-- it's called "The
Marketing Analytics Starter

00:33:48.870 --> 00:33:49.480
Pack."

00:33:49.480 --> 00:33:52.440
It's showing you how to test
whether or not what you built

00:33:52.440 --> 00:33:55.080
is effective using
Google Analytics.

00:33:55.080 --> 00:33:58.740
So you can imagine if a
user read these two comments

00:33:58.740 --> 00:34:00.570
or clicked on them
and made a purchasing

00:34:00.570 --> 00:34:03.150
decision, through Google
Analytics I could track all

00:34:03.150 --> 00:34:06.780
that click stream, do
some basic A/B testing,

00:34:06.780 --> 00:34:09.929
and say yes, this model that
we can came up with this way

00:34:09.929 --> 00:34:12.600
is the most effective
one to drive sales.

00:34:15.310 --> 00:34:18.989
All right, let's get back
into the slides, and wrap up.

00:34:27.090 --> 00:34:29.790
Real quick note-- so
the Sirocco library

00:34:29.790 --> 00:34:31.925
that I used is Java based.

00:34:31.925 --> 00:34:32.550
You can go out.

00:34:32.550 --> 00:34:33.460
It's open source.

00:34:33.460 --> 00:34:35.210
You can find it in GitHub.

00:34:35.210 --> 00:34:39.360
And it talks about how you
can use various indexing

00:34:39.360 --> 00:34:41.650
techniques, and how you can
leverage it with Dataflow.

00:34:41.650 --> 00:34:43.080
But you don't have to
use it with Dataflow.

00:34:43.080 --> 00:34:45.820
If you're running this inside of
Apache Spark or something else,

00:34:45.820 --> 00:34:47.219
or maybe you could use
it-- you would even

00:34:47.219 --> 00:34:49.020
want to run it locally
on your own machine,

00:34:49.020 --> 00:34:51.909
although that wouldn't be
great for a big data problem.

00:34:51.909 --> 00:34:53.909
So I encourage you to go
out and look at Sirocco

00:34:53.909 --> 00:34:55.950
if you're trying to look
at the emotion analysis.

00:34:55.950 --> 00:34:59.970
If you just need sentiment
information and/or

00:34:59.970 --> 00:35:02.820
the lexical graph, go ahead
and use Cloud Natural Language

00:35:02.820 --> 00:35:03.390
Processing.

00:35:03.390 --> 00:35:05.980
It is a great
technique if you need

00:35:05.980 --> 00:35:08.830
that higher-level information.

00:35:08.830 --> 00:35:10.170
So what did we show you?

00:35:10.170 --> 00:35:12.810
The journey was go ahead and
ingest, fire up BigQuery.

00:35:12.810 --> 00:35:13.890
It's super simple.

00:35:13.890 --> 00:35:15.960
You basically say
"Create data sets,

00:35:15.960 --> 00:35:17.130
go ahead and load tables."

00:35:17.130 --> 00:35:20.640
And you can either do that
in batch or streaming mode.

00:35:20.640 --> 00:35:22.590
BigQuery enables you
to ingest the data

00:35:22.590 --> 00:35:27.510
in real time, up to 100,000
records per second per table.

00:35:27.510 --> 00:35:30.870
And then go ahead and
do some quick analysis

00:35:30.870 --> 00:35:34.765
inside of Datalab or
inside of Data Studio.

00:35:34.765 --> 00:35:36.390
We talked about doing
some enrichments.

00:35:36.390 --> 00:35:38.730
So you could use the Cloud
Natural Language API.

00:35:38.730 --> 00:35:40.860
You could also use
our Vision API.

00:35:40.860 --> 00:35:43.110
You could do any type
of enrichment technique

00:35:43.110 --> 00:35:48.330
that you want using Dataflow
or another ETL tool.

00:35:48.330 --> 00:35:50.020
And then we get into
the analysis piece.

00:35:50.020 --> 00:35:53.460
So you could do something like
with Sirocco, you could write

00:35:53.460 --> 00:35:57.709
your own analysis algorithms.

00:35:57.709 --> 00:35:59.625
And then once you get
into feature development

00:35:59.625 --> 00:36:02.650
and modeling, you can do
that inside of a Datalab.

00:36:02.650 --> 00:36:04.260
You can do inside of IPython.

00:36:04.260 --> 00:36:07.220
You can do it inside of
R. It doesn't matter,

00:36:07.220 --> 00:36:09.210
to go ahead and create
the types of features

00:36:09.210 --> 00:36:11.320
that you want in order
to build a model.

00:36:11.320 --> 00:36:13.920
And then finally, if you need
to go ahead and train and serve

00:36:13.920 --> 00:36:17.400
this at scale, you can go ahead
and use Cloud Machine Learning

00:36:17.400 --> 00:36:19.170
Engine.

00:36:19.170 --> 00:36:20.580
So we went through a bunch.

00:36:20.580 --> 00:36:24.240
But this is, in essence, how we
got back to this architecture.

00:36:24.240 --> 00:36:25.920
But now this has
been contextualized

00:36:25.920 --> 00:36:26.847
in a retail scenario.

00:36:26.847 --> 00:36:28.680
But this would work in
any type of scenario.

00:36:28.680 --> 00:36:31.050
This could be for life sciences.

00:36:31.050 --> 00:36:32.790
It could be for
financial services.

00:36:32.790 --> 00:36:34.770
It could be for doing
analysis over sports.

00:36:37.680 --> 00:36:42.107
So with that, Sap, I want
to thank you for your time.

00:36:42.107 --> 00:36:43.440
SAPTARSHI MUKHERJEE: Absolutely.

00:36:43.440 --> 00:36:47.490
ERIC SCHMIDT: We've kind of
gone from zero to emotions.

00:36:47.490 --> 00:36:49.860
And we understand
how to quantify love.

00:36:49.860 --> 00:37:02.440
And with that, we're going to
flip over to the live Q and A.

00:37:02.440 --> 00:37:07.370
[SIDE CONVERSATION]

00:38:02.956 --> 00:38:05.330
SAPTARSHI MUKHERJEE: So Eric,
we talked about e-commerce.

00:38:05.330 --> 00:38:08.390
And in real businesses, you
will have hundreds of thousands

00:38:08.390 --> 00:38:11.330
of customers leaving
comments on your site,

00:38:11.330 --> 00:38:13.860
and expressing their
emotions and sentiments.

00:38:13.860 --> 00:38:17.420
How can I use Cloud Natural
Language API to score

00:38:17.420 --> 00:38:19.149
lots and lots of comments?

00:38:19.149 --> 00:38:19.940
ERIC SCHMIDT: Sure.

00:38:19.940 --> 00:38:23.450
So there are several techniques.

00:38:23.450 --> 00:38:25.710
The one that we talked
about was ultimately,

00:38:25.710 --> 00:38:28.321
you want to do this in the
most parallelized fashion as

00:38:28.321 --> 00:38:28.820
possible.

00:38:28.820 --> 00:38:32.030
Because you have lots of things,
and if you did them serially,

00:38:32.030 --> 00:38:33.720
it would take you lots of time.

00:38:33.720 --> 00:38:39.530
So using a product like Cloud
Dataflow or another ETL tool.

00:38:39.530 --> 00:38:42.710
So maybe you wanted to use Spark
or just a straight MapReduce

00:38:42.710 --> 00:38:43.700
job.

00:38:43.700 --> 00:38:47.420
You could go ahead and
build a graph that will then

00:38:47.420 --> 00:38:49.700
go ahead and
parallelize the analysis

00:38:49.700 --> 00:38:51.110
of each one of those comments.

00:38:51.110 --> 00:38:52.693
And that's the
technique that we used.

00:38:52.693 --> 00:38:55.460
Here we just used
the cloud NLP API,

00:38:55.460 --> 00:38:58.370
and then build a function
that says, OK, input comment,

00:38:58.370 --> 00:39:00.440
call function, and then
you parallelize that.

00:39:00.440 --> 00:39:03.290
So you could effectively do
a job that would, say, take

00:39:03.290 --> 00:39:07.430
24 hours in total.

00:39:07.430 --> 00:39:10.725
You could run that job in
six minutes of clock time.

00:39:10.725 --> 00:39:11.850
SAPTARSHI MUKHERJEE: Great.

00:39:11.850 --> 00:39:13.266
And what if I need
to do something

00:39:13.266 --> 00:39:17.410
more custom than what Cloud
Natural language API offers?

00:39:17.410 --> 00:39:18.860
ERIC SCHMIDT: Yeah,
good question.

00:39:18.860 --> 00:39:21.200
So Cloud NLP, as
we showed you, you

00:39:21.200 --> 00:39:25.370
can get document and
sentence-level sentiments.

00:39:25.370 --> 00:39:30.170
You can also understand
the syntax tree.

00:39:30.170 --> 00:39:32.600
If you need more,
there are a bunch

00:39:32.600 --> 00:39:36.290
of already open-source libraries
out there, like Sirocco.

00:39:36.290 --> 00:39:38.860
And these, in some cases,
play well with each other.

00:39:38.860 --> 00:39:42.490
So in fact, with Sirocco, I was
using the output of Cloud NLP

00:39:42.490 --> 00:39:45.710
in order to drive Sirocco.

00:39:45.710 --> 00:39:48.380
Or you could build
your own classifier.

00:39:48.380 --> 00:39:49.880
You could, say,
build something that

00:39:49.880 --> 00:39:55.190
was more focused on identifying
the subject of that emotion,

00:39:55.190 --> 00:39:55.850
if you will.

00:39:55.850 --> 00:39:59.810
And the point being is that
anything that you do custom,

00:39:59.810 --> 00:40:03.260
you can then load it back into
the data transformation or ETL

00:40:03.260 --> 00:40:05.420
system that you're
using, say, for example,

00:40:05.420 --> 00:40:06.952
like Cloud Dataflow.

00:40:06.952 --> 00:40:09.160
SAPTARSHI MUKHERJEE: Can we
apply this same technique

00:40:09.160 --> 00:40:10.345
to do Twitter analytics?

00:40:10.345 --> 00:40:14.810
Like customers are talking
about my brand in social media

00:40:14.810 --> 00:40:16.610
platforms like Twitter.

00:40:16.610 --> 00:40:18.502
Can I apply the same
techniques to understand

00:40:18.502 --> 00:40:20.710
the emotions and the sentiment
of the Twitter streams

00:40:20.710 --> 00:40:21.530
that are coming?

00:40:21.530 --> 00:40:22.821
ERIC SCHMIDT: Yeah, definitely.

00:40:22.821 --> 00:40:27.560
And the path that one
would take is basically

00:40:27.560 --> 00:40:29.500
ingesting that stream.

00:40:29.500 --> 00:40:30.750
So Twitter is a great example.

00:40:30.750 --> 00:40:33.410
So if you have access
to the full Firehose,

00:40:33.410 --> 00:40:36.625
or maybe even to
the Decahose you

00:40:36.625 --> 00:40:38.000
need to be able
to go in, ingest,

00:40:38.000 --> 00:40:43.740
and put that data someplace
in a very low latent process.

00:40:43.740 --> 00:40:45.365
BigQuery is a great
place to do that.

00:40:45.365 --> 00:40:47.990
So I can basically go ahead and
read from those Twitter streams

00:40:47.990 --> 00:40:51.800
raw, and ingest in real
time into BigQuery,

00:40:51.800 --> 00:40:55.370
and then turn around and
write your real-time analysis

00:40:55.370 --> 00:40:56.185
on top of it.

00:40:56.185 --> 00:40:57.560
SAPTARSHI MUKHERJEE:
And BigQuery

00:40:57.560 --> 00:40:59.726
allows you to do streaming
analytics, pretty much.

00:40:59.726 --> 00:41:01.100
Do I need to do
any configuration

00:41:01.100 --> 00:41:02.100
to make it happen?

00:41:02.100 --> 00:41:02.808
ERIC SCHMIDT: No.

00:41:02.808 --> 00:41:05.786
You literally just create
a table and start writing.

00:41:05.786 --> 00:41:07.410
SAPTARSHI MUKHERJEE:
Wow, that's great.

00:41:07.410 --> 00:41:09.530
And you showed Data Lab.

00:41:09.530 --> 00:41:13.280
But if I'm an R user and I
want to use any other tool,

00:41:13.280 --> 00:41:17.660
how do I connect to
BigQuery to analyze my data?

00:41:17.660 --> 00:41:23.330
ERIC SCHMIDT: There are two
open source libraries right now

00:41:23.330 --> 00:41:24.780
to connect to BigQuery.

00:41:24.780 --> 00:41:30.980
And most recently, pandas also
just added native read support.

00:41:30.980 --> 00:41:34.480
So now you can just go do pandas
read from Google BigQuery.

00:41:34.480 --> 00:41:36.740
And all the underlying
connectivity, et cetera

00:41:36.740 --> 00:41:38.090
is handled by the library.

00:41:38.090 --> 00:41:40.298
So if you're in any type of
environment that supports

00:41:40.298 --> 00:41:41.960
pandas, that will work nicely.

00:41:41.960 --> 00:41:44.265
And there's also the
similar API for R.

00:41:44.265 --> 00:41:45.890
SAPTARSHI MUKHERJEE:
Wow, that's great.

00:41:45.890 --> 00:41:48.331
So no need to take, like, a
data export to a separate data

00:41:48.331 --> 00:41:48.830
store.

00:41:48.830 --> 00:41:50.060
You just directly do it off of--

00:41:50.060 --> 00:41:50.768
ERIC SCHMIDT: No.

00:41:50.768 --> 00:41:55.940
If you're moving text
files around like that,

00:41:55.940 --> 00:41:57.050
go find those libraries.

00:41:57.050 --> 00:41:58.610
They're quite easy to use.

00:41:58.610 --> 00:42:00.526
SAPTARSHI MUKHERJEE: I
think the last question

00:42:00.526 --> 00:42:04.270
we're going to take is, how do
I find more granular sentiment?

00:42:04.270 --> 00:42:07.170
I think the question is, how
do I understand emotions?

00:42:07.170 --> 00:42:10.060
I think you talked about
the open source library.

00:42:10.060 --> 00:42:12.020
But can you just
re-emphasize that, please?

00:42:12.020 --> 00:42:14.416
How do you do--

00:42:14.416 --> 00:42:16.040
ERIC SCHMIDT: There
are other libraries

00:42:16.040 --> 00:42:17.780
out there other than Sirocco.

00:42:17.780 --> 00:42:21.230
But in the case of Sirocco,
the builder of it--

00:42:21.230 --> 00:42:24.860
his name's Sergei
Sokolenko-- he's a Googler.

00:42:24.860 --> 00:42:27.200
And he and I had several
lunches about this topic.

00:42:27.200 --> 00:42:30.530
And it was just like he had
the existing-- or the analysis

00:42:30.530 --> 00:42:33.420
that Plutchik built and
the research that he build

00:42:33.420 --> 00:42:35.360
was fairly sound.

00:42:35.360 --> 00:42:38.360
It was very well received,
and it works well

00:42:38.360 --> 00:42:40.320
for lots of business domains.

00:42:40.320 --> 00:42:43.310
So we whenever I was
making a decision,

00:42:43.310 --> 00:42:45.740
I didn't necessarily
need to do more.

00:42:45.740 --> 00:42:48.230
It provided the right
level of granularity.

00:42:48.230 --> 00:42:51.050
What I needed was make
sure that it was accurate.

00:42:51.050 --> 00:42:54.920
In my case, overall, it was.

00:42:54.920 --> 00:42:57.650
So you kind of pick the
tool that you need in order

00:42:57.650 --> 00:42:58.730
to solve the problem.

00:42:58.730 --> 00:43:01.430
But you may find that you need
more granularity out there.

00:43:01.430 --> 00:43:04.497
And there are other open source
libraries that you could use.

00:43:04.497 --> 00:43:06.080
SAPTARSHI MUKHERJEE:
